The '''Western Kaftian Tradition''' (also known as the '''Coastal-Hill Complex''') is a distinct {{W|archaeological culture}} that flourished along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast of southwestern [[Southwestern Kaftia]] during the prehistoric and early historic periods. Isolated from the northern savanna by the [[Kurinka Hills]] and from the southern highlands by the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], the society that produced it developed a maritime orientation centred on fishing, megalithic construction, and the monopoly of the [[Cowrie|cowrie shell]] trade. Its modern descendants are the [[Fayehan people|Fayehan]] and [[Kijuran people|Kijuran]] peoples.

== Geography and isolation ==
[[File:Kurinka_Hills_Coast.jpg|thumb|right|The [[Kurinka Hills]] viewed from the [[Fayeha]] coast, the natural barrier that isolated the Western Tradition from the northern savanna.]]
The Western Tradition developed along the rugged southwest coast of the peninsula, in the territory of modern [[Fayeha]] and [[Kijura]]. The region receives heavy rainfall from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and is characterised by temperate rainforests and rocky promontories. To the north, the [[Kurinka Hills]] — a low ridge of 600–1,000 m — separate the coast from the open savanna; to the east, the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] blocks access to the southern highlands. This double barrier allowed the Western peoples to develop in relative isolation from both the [[Northern Kaftian Tradition]] and the [[Southern Kaftian Tradition]] for several millennia.

== Archaeological features ==

The material culture of the Western Tradition is defined by two principal categories of monument: megalithic stone structures and shell middens.

=== Megalithic structures ===
Between approximately 3,000 and 1,000 BCE, the inhabitants of the coast constructed elaborate megalithic monuments. These include large stone circles, often aligned with celestial events such as the solstices or the rising of specific stars used in navigation; single standing stones ({{W|menhir|menhirs}}), some exceeding 5 metres in height, which are believed to have marked territorial boundaries or served as memorials for those lost at sea; and stone burial chambers ({{W|dolmen|dolmens}}) covered with earth, distinct from the earthen tumuli of the [[Northern Kaftian Tradition|Northern Tradition]].

=== Shell middens ===
[[File:Western_Shell_Midden.jpg|thumb|left|Cross-section of a shell midden in [[Kijura]], showing layers of mollusc shells interspersed with human burials and stone tools.]]
The most ubiquitous features of the Western archaeological landscape are enormous {{W|midden|shell middens}}, mounds of accumulated mollusc shells that in some cases reach heights of over 10 metres. Built up over thousands of years, the largest middens served as foundations for later settlements. Excavation has revealed that these sites were not solely refuse deposits but held ritual significance: many contain human burials and offerings of polished stone tools interspersed among the shell layers.

== Economy and trade ==

The Western economy was almost entirely maritime. The diet consisted primarily of fish, shellfish (oysters, mussels, and clams), and sea turtles, supplemented by small-scale horticulture focused on indigenous tubers and oil palms. Coastal lagoons were modified into evaporation pans for the production of sea salt, which was traded to the iron-producing societies of the interior.

The most significant economic feature of the Western Tradition was the harvest and export of the '''{{W|cowrie}} shell'''. Found in abundance along the rocky [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast, these shells were polished and traded inland to the [[Northern Kaftian Tradition|Northern]] and [[Southern Kaftian Tradition|Southern]] peoples, where they became the standard unit of currency for centuries. Control of this trade brought considerable wealth to the coastal communities, as evidenced by the presence of exotic goods — including northern copper and southern iron — in high-status burial sites.

== Social structure and religion ==

Archaeological and anthropological evidence suggests a society markedly different from its neighbours in several respects. Unlike the patrilineal clans of the North and South, Western society appears to have been {{W|matrilineality|matrilineal}}, tracing descent and inheritance through the female line. This structure may reflect the extended absences of men engaged in deep-sea fishing voyages.

Religious life centred on the veneration of the '''Mother of Waters''', a deity personifying the ocean. Rituals involved casting offerings of pearls and polished stones into the sea to ensure safe voyages and productive fishing seasons. Evidence from excavated stone circles points to a class of female ritual specialists who maintained the megalithic monuments and the astronomical calendars associated with them.

== Technology ==
[[File:Western_Dugout_Canoe.jpg|thumb|right|Reconstruction of a Western Tradition ocean-going dugout canoe, capable of carrying 20–30 people.]]
The Western peoples constructed large, ocean-going dugout canoes from the hardwoods of the coastal forests. These vessels, capable of carrying 20 to 30 people, enabled long-distance coastal trade and deep-sea fishing. Navigational techniques based on wave patterns, bird migrations, and star positions were developed long before contact with the [[Mesogean Sea]] civilisations. The Western Tradition did not develop metalworking independently; metal tools arrived only with the expansion of the [[Burawa|Burawi Empire]] in the medieval period.

